      {
        "id": 204965,
        "name": "MindWanderer",
        "username": "MindWanderer",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/mindwanderer/{size}/4816_2.png",
        "created_at": "2017-10-02T19:07:37.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Chaosmancer\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/chaosmancer/48/1977_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Chaosmancer:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Honestly, I think Christopher kind of gets to cheat in this department as compared to Adam. As the \"Story guy\" to a story that is largely nebulous and ill-defined at times (What exactly did Judge Mental say that pushed IDealist over the edge instead of sealing the break-up of the Sentinels?) almost all the minor details that make or break the type of crtique that Adam is applying to his art is invisible or non-existent.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>From the perspective of The Letters Page, sure.&nbsp; But he's also the main game designer, so he has to live with&nbsp;<em>those</em> mistakes as well.&nbsp; The core game got to benefit from being revised in the Enhanced Edition (and Adam got to make updates then, too), but there are some… questionable… design decisions that are still with us, and he just has to live with those.&nbsp; For instance, I'm sure he'd make some changes to Rook City if for some reason it ever made sense to revise and reprint it.</p>",
        "post_number": 33,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T05:34:12.496Z",
